// Regression test (LANG-004 item 3): `extern fn` may now be declared
// INSIDE a function body, not just at file/top level. Previously rejected
// with a misleading diagnostic: the parser would actually consume the
// `extern fn ...;` declaration fine (ParseExternDecl() already eats its
// own trailing ';'), then choke on the FOLLOWING statement with "Expected
// ';' after statement." -- because ParseBlock()'s generic expression-
// statement fallback expected to consume its own trailing ';' too, and it
// had already been eaten. See ParseBlock()'s TOK_EXTERN branch.
//
// A function-local extern's symbol isn't hoisted by Pass 1 (hoistTopLevel()
// never enters a function body -- that's the entire reason hoisting exists
// as a separate pass, see docs/architecture/compiler_pipeline.md §3), so it
// must be registered live, the moment Pass 2 walks over it
// (Resolver::visitExternFuncDecl(), gated on FunctionNestingDepth_ > 0).
// This test's real job is proving that registration actually happens --
// merely accepting the syntax without ever being able to CALL the result
// would be a much less useful fix than the one line count suggests.

import test;

fn main(): int {
    extern fn abs(x: int): int;
    test::assert(abs(-42) == 42, "a function-local extern fn must be resolvable and callable");
    return 0;
}
